;; FH-ROOT-008 — the client-only boundary, and the one flip that completes it.
;;
;; A browser-only subtree is the one region of a page a server cannot render.
;; The boundary makes that a DECLARED fact rather than a runtime accident: the
;; author states what stands in the region's place everywhere the client
;; runtime is absent, and the fallback is mandatory precisely because the
;; alternative — a hole in the server's output that fills in from nowhere — is
;; what the boundary exists to prevent.
;;
;; What completes it is the phase. The structural render on either host is
;; `:server` phase and produces the fallback. An ordinary mount is born
;; `:client` and produces the client subtree on its first and only render — so
;; a page that never server-renders pays nothing for the boundary and behaves
;; exactly as it did before the boundary existed. A HYDRATING root is the only
;; case with two renders in it, and the ordering of those two is the whole of
;; this row.
;;
;; It boots `:server`, so React adopts the server's own fallback markup
;; against a client render that produces the same markup. THEN it flips. The
;; ordering holds by construction rather than by care — the flip is a
;; post-commit passive effect, so it cannot run until React has committed and
;; so adopted the `:server`-phase render. A flip that beat that check would
;; swap in a tree the server never rendered and manufacture a mismatch against
;; a page that was never wrong. The `:different-client-subtree` case below is
;; the falsifier: its client subtrees deliberately DISAGREE with its
;; fallbacks, so an implementation that flipped early would report a mismatch
;; here and this row would go red.
;;
;; ONE root-scoped write, and the fixture is shaped to prove it. Two sites,
;; not one: a per-site implementation would swap them across two updates, and
;; a page with several client-only regions would tear — some regions live
;; while their neighbours still show fallbacks. Reading both sites after the
;; flip, and counting exactly one flip trace for the root, is what a
;; single-site fixture could never falsify.
{:fh/id "FH-ROOT-008"
 :fh/law
 "An interpreted `v/client-only` site renders its MANDATORY capability-free fallback in `:server` phase — the structural render on either host, and a hydrating root's first render — and its client subtree in `:client` phase; a hydrating root flips `:server` -> `:client` exactly once, strictly AFTER the adoption commit, and that ONE root-scoped write swaps EVERY site in the root in a single update with no per-site state, while a non-hydrating mount is born `:client` and never flips."

 ;; --- the structural render, on both hosts -------------------------------
 ;;
 ;; Only the fallback is walked. The client subtree is a value the structural
 ;; walk deliberately never enters, and the fragment marker is what says the
 ;; markup standing here is a stand-in rather than the real thing.
 :structural
 {:props {:left "live" :right "live"}
  :tree  {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.client-only-views/two-sites
          :props   {:left "live" :right "live"}
          :children
          [{:tag :section
            :attrs {:id "panel"}
            :children
            [{:tag :h1 :attrs {:id "heading"} :children ["Dashboard"]}
             {:rf.ui/boundary :client-only
              :children [{:tag :p :attrs {:id "left"} :children ["left-fallback"]}]}
             {:rf.ui/boundary :client-only
              :children [{:tag :p :attrs {:id "right"} :children ["right-fallback"]}]}]}]}}

 ;; A view whose whole body IS a client-only site keeps its marker: the
 ;; boundary wraps the fallback node rather than adopting its children, which
 ;; is the one shape in which the marker could silently be dropped.
 :site-rooted
 {:tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.client-only-views/site-rooted
         :children [{:rf.ui/boundary :client-only
                     :children [{:tag :span :attrs {:class "stand-in"}
                                 :children ["stand in"]}]}]}}

 ;; --- the refusals -------------------------------------------------------
 ;;
 ;; The fallback is mandatory, the option roster is closed, and both are
 ;; refused at the call rather than rendered as an absence.
 :refusals
 [{:note  "no options map at all"
   :opts  nil
   :error :rf.error/ui-tree-malformed}
  {:note  "an options map with no :fallback"
   :opts  {}
   :error :rf.error/ui-tree-malformed}
  {:note  "an explicit nil :fallback is PRESENCE — it renders nothing, and says so"
   :opts  {:fallback nil}
   :error :accepted}
  {:note  "an unknown option"
   :opts  {:fallback [:i "x"] :timeout-ms 300}
   :error :rf.error/ui-tree-malformed}]

 ;; --- the browser: a non-hydrating mount ---------------------------------
 ;;
 ;; Born `:client`. The client subtree is what the first and only render
 ;; produces, and NO flip trace fires: there is no fallback pass to flip away
 ;; from, so a root that emitted one would be reporting a transition that did
 ;; not happen.
 :mount
 {:props     {:left "A" :right "B"}
  :text      {"#left" "left-A" "#right" "right-B"}
  :flips     0}

 ;; --- the browser: the hydrating root ------------------------------------
 ;;
 ;; The server rendered the FALLBACKS, because the server render is `:server`
 ;; phase. The manifest names an id this root form would never derive, so the
 ;; identity assertions are reading the wire rather than a coincidence.
 :hydrate
 {:props    {:left "A" :right "B"}
  :manifest {:rf.root/schema-version 1
             :root-id           :fh.root/phase-flip
             :identifier-prefix "srv8-"}
  :root-id  :fh.root/phase-flip
  :server-html
  "<section id=\"panel\"><h1 id=\"heading\">Dashboard</h1><p id=\"left\">left-fallback</p><p id=\"right\">right-fallback</p></section>"

  ;; The adoption pass: the server's own nodes survive, which is what proves
  ;; the first client render produced the fallbacks rather than the client
  ;; subtrees. A root that booted `:client` would have replaced these.
  :adopted-nodes ["#heading" "#left" "#right"]

  ;; After the flip: BOTH sites live, read together. One update, no tearing.
  :flipped-text {"#left" "left-A" "#right" "right-B"}

  ;; The heading is outside both boundaries and is untouched by the flip.
  :stable-text {"#heading" "Dashboard"}

  ;; Exactly one flip trace for this root, carrying its id.
  :flips 1
  :flip-operation :rf.ssr/phase-flip

  ;; And the flip is not a mismatch: the client subtrees DISAGREE with the
  ;; fallbacks by construction, so an early flip would be reported here.
  :mismatches 0}}
